application secrets?

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.890
How is that even possible?

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.770
CRAIG LABENZ: It's
possible by the magic of,

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:35.570
we'll probably have
to make a few edits

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.460
to your settings.py file.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.700
But I've been using it on my
own web apps for a bit now.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.160
Here, let me run it real quick
to redeploy my application,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.836
and then we can integrate
it into your project.

00:01:46.836 --> 00:01:49.400
MARTIN OMANDER:
That sounds great.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.560
Oh, look, there it goes.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:54.400
Wow.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.890
You and I just stood
here like bumps on a log

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:58.915
while the script did everything.

00:01:58.915 --> 00:01:59.800
CRAIG LABENZ: I know.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.912
Wasn't it great?

00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:02.620
I'll send you the link
to the repository.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:04.620
And after you clone it,
you can walk through it.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.120
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
I have the code up.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.720
I've seen a little Terraform
before, but I'm not an expert.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.490
What am I looking at here?

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:14.860
CRAIG LABENZ: Terraform
is an open-source

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.350
infrastructure-as-code
tool that allows

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.630
for consistent,
declarative workflows

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:23.605
to manage large-scale
application infrastructure.

00:02:23.605 --> 00:02:24.730
That's a bit of a mouthful.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:26.188
But in short, it
just means you get

00:02:26.188 --> 00:02:28.290
to write down what servers
your application needs

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.040
and how they should
connect to each other.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.115
And Terraform figures out
how to make that a reality.

00:02:33.115 --> 00:02:36.250
MARTIN OMANDER: And it knows
how to talk with Google Cloud?

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.480
CRAIG LABENZ: It sure does.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:39.070
Looking up at the
top of the file,

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:42.400
the first block specifies a
dependency on the Google Cloud

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.600
platform bindings.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.450
So with that, Terraform
downloads the necessary code

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.640
to translate everything
that we write here

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.350
into Google Cloud API calls.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:51.430
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:53.110
OK, I'm hooked.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.838
Can you walk me
through this file?

00:02:54.838 --> 00:02:55.630
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:57.790
So the next step-- make
environment variables

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:00.610
locally accessible to
your Terraform workflow.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.110
You can hard-code
anything that's

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.300
fixed to be default
values and then leave

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.780
the rest to be passed in
from the command line.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.500
MARTIN OMANDER: OK,
that makes sense.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.015
And what does the
script do next?

00:03:10.015 --> 00:03:12.640
CRAIG LABENZ: If you've ever
configured a new GCP project

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.470
by hand in the Web
Console, you've

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.230
encountered the process of
activating specific service

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.010
APIs.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:20.650
So this next part looks
ahead of the complete set

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.967
of service APIs it will
require and turns them all on.

00:03:23.967 --> 00:03:26.050
We'll also want to set up
a custom service account

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.330
to run our Cloud Run service.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:31.070
The default service account has
access to do a lot of things.

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.040
So I usually create
my own service account

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.127
and specifically set what
it can and cannot do.

00:03:36.127 --> 00:03:37.960
MARTIN OMANDER: I love
that this script even

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.360
captures the principle
of least privilege.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:41.680
CRAIG LABENZ: Always.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.870
Now, the next step
takes some time

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.510
to complete the first time
you run this configuration,

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.643
because it initializes an entire
Cloud SQL Postgres instance.

00:03:50.643 --> 00:03:52.060
MARTIN OMANDER:
This section looks

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:55.030
like it does a lot
of heavy lifting

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:58.460
I see the creation of
a Cloud SQL instance--

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.180
let's see, a user, and
a specific password.

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.267
But how do we get that
password into our code?

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:06.850
CRAIG LABENZ: Hold
on to that thought,

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.230
because it is coming soon.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.090
But first, there's
a section that

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.670
sets up the final
project-specific values we

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.300
have, our admin user's password
and a Google Cloud Storage

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.835
bucket to host our
static and media files.

00:04:18.835 --> 00:04:20.260
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, neat.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.510
I like that Terraform
generates these secrets,

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:24.320
so they never
exist in plaintext.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:25.840
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah, exactly.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.480
And now that Terraform has
generated everything sensitive,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.120
we can prepare to securely
get all of that information

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.170
into our Django settings.

00:04:33.170 --> 00:04:35.830
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, that
one is a little dense.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:37.510
Can you walk me
through it, Craig?

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:38.650
CRAIG LABENZ: Absolutely.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.370
This block uses a simple
template, which I'll send you,

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:43.840
to create an
environment variable

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.780
file with our database
credentials, secret key,

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:48.130
and storage bucket.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.500
Later, we'll make an edit
to your settings.py file

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.135
to actually read
in those values.

00:04:52.135 --> 00:04:54.220
MARTIN OMANDER:
Perfect, because I

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.350
have flagged some
to-dos in my settings

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.570
file for each of those things.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:05:00.156
What's next here?

00:05:00.156 --> 00:05:02.090
CRAIG LABENZ: So far,
we've generated values

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:03.705
for a few sensitive
pieces of our app.

00:05:03.705 --> 00:05:05.330
But Terraform has
yet to actually share

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.860
that information with anyone.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:09.440
So this part creates a new
entry in Secret Manager

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.335
and gives access to the
necessary service accounts.

00:05:12.335 --> 00:05:15.720
MARTIN OMANDER: I see
this creating new secrets.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.808
But have our secret values been
uploaded to Secret Manager yet?

00:05:19.808 --> 00:05:20.975
CRAIG LABENZ: Yes, good eye.

00:05:20.975 --> 00:05:22.880
If our Terraform
manifest ended here,

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.790
we would, in fact, have some
empty and pretty useless

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.290
entries in Secret Manager.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:29.240
But that's where this
next part comes in,

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.295
to gather up all of the
values we care about

00:05:31.295 --> 00:05:32.420
and populate those secrets.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.320
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:35.510
And I'm guessing that
our Django app will then

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:39.160
need to access these
secrets during startup?

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.660
CRAIG LABENZ: You're really
quite a good guesser, Martin.

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.205
Has anyone ever told you that?

00:05:43.205 --> 00:05:45.612
MARTIN OMANDER: Aw, shucks.

00:05:45.612 --> 00:05:48.070
CRAIG LABENZ: So this is where
that template file comes in,

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:50.440
because the structure of
the secrets that we uploaded

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:53.380
isn't just the values awkwardly
placed in the text file,

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.900
but instead are valid
environment variables file

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.840
that our application can source,
thus allowing your Django

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:01.910
code to pull those values
right out of the environment.

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:02.880
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.410
Terraform's thought
of everything.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:08.925
And now we're getting to the
Cloud Run service definition.

00:06:08.925 --> 00:06:10.020
CRAIG LABENZ: Indeed.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:12.750
It's taken a lot to get here,
but the Terraform manifest

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:17.460
is now finally ready to define
our actual Cloud Run service.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:19.890
MARTIN OMANDER: I also see
another IAM setting there?

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:20.682
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah.

00:06:20.682 --> 00:06:22.620
There's always more IAM setting.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.380
In the Google Cloud Console,
there's a checkbox that says,

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:26.910
"Make this service public."

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.800
And the GCloud CLI, it's the
--allow-unauthenticated flag.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.410
In short, these two
just open up our service

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.545
to the entire internet, which
is, of course, what we want.

00:06:37.545 --> 00:06:39.840
MARTIN OMANDER: And
speaking of policy, it

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.330
looks like you're also
setting service permissions.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.105
And is that a for loop?

00:06:45.105 --> 00:06:46.465
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah, it is.

00:06:46.465 --> 00:06:48.090
Terraform allows you
to perform actions

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:49.500
against a list of values.

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:51.480
In this case, we're
giving both Cloud Build

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:54.870
and our actual Django service
account access to Cloud Run

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.325
and to Cloud SQL.

00:06:56.325 --> 00:06:59.490
MARTIN OMANDER: But why
does Cloud Build need access

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.750
to the database?

00:07:00.750 --> 00:07:02.580
CRAIG LABENZ: Well,
that's a good question.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:06.390
As most Django developers
know, some of your deployments

00:07:06.390 --> 00:07:08.790
involve schema migrations
that must be applied

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.460
running manage.py migrate.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.440
The only problem is,
Cloud Run has nowhere

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.460
to run that one-off command.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.850
So to solve this, I've
set up a series of steps

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:21.180
in the cloudbuild-migrate.yaml
script to run against

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.500
our container image.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:24.840
One of these is the
famous migrate command.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:29.495
And thus Cloud Build, will also
require access to the database.

00:07:29.495 --> 00:07:31.620
MARTIN OMANDER: So is that
your continuous delivery

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.790
configuration?

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:34.170
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah, it is.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:36.660
And you can run this manually
or as part of a deployment

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:38.580
to automatically run
the steps that you need

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:40.847
to migrate your application,
or any other commands

00:07:40.847 --> 00:07:41.430
that you need.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:42.240
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:43.890
There's one last
part of the Terraform

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.490
script that we haven't covered,
these output parts here.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:48.375
What are they?

00:07:48.375 --> 00:07:50.250
CRAIG LABENZ: Those are
values that Terraform

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.780
will display once it's
finished applying everything.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.690
And these are the useful
values that we need,

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:57.505
our service URL and
our superuser password.

00:07:57.505 --> 00:07:59.880
Of course, we've defined our
password as being sensitive,

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.190
so it will only output that
if we explicitly ask for it.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:05.520
We could get those values
by running GCloud commands

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.110
against Cloud Run
and Secret Manager,

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.760
but this saves some time.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:09.780
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.025
This is all so declarative.

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.610
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah,
that's the point.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:16.860
Terraform allows you to
capture your entire deployment

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.320
process in one
declarative manifest,

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.350
and it handles the rest.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:24.630
MARTIN OMANDER: Now, all that
mumbo-jumbo about consistent,

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:27.580
declarative infrastructure
really makes sense.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:31.935
So with all this, I can go
edit my settings.py file now.

00:08:31.935 --> 00:08:33.210
CRAIG LABENZ: Sure can.

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.970
Open up that file, and
we'll get started editing.

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:38.760
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I'm
in my settings.py file,

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:42.708
and I'm ready to grab my
passwords from Secret Manager.

00:08:42.708 --> 00:08:43.770
CRAIG LABENZ: Great.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.270
I'm sending you a
snippet now, which

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:48.165
you can drop in at the top of
your file, pretty much as is.

00:08:48.165 --> 00:08:50.910
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh, this is hefty.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:51.990
Let's see.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:56.340
That read_env function will
read from a local environment,

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.420
plus a .Env file, if I have one.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.850
But then we get
to this try block.

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:04.590
Let's see.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:07.980
We attempt to use the Google
Auth API to load our default

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.080
credentials in production.

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.440
I know these are provided
by Cloud Run itself.

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.350
If they're missing, we must
be doing local development,

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.420
so it quietly fails?

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:20.590
CRAIG LABENZ: Yep, you're
on the right track.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.200
This setup handles
both production

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.116
and local development,
which is nice.

00:09:25.116 --> 00:09:29.520
MARTIN OMANDER: And then, if
it sees we have a project ID,

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.940
we use the Secret Manager API
to get those very same secrets

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.340
we saved in Terraform manifest.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:38.400
And then, with those values, it
loads them into the environment

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.455
as well?

00:09:39.455 --> 00:09:40.930
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah, precisely.

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:44.470
And instead of accessing those
variables via os.environ,

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.480
like you might be used
to, I do recommend

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:49.855
using the environment library
to streamline the process.

00:09:49.855 --> 00:09:52.060
MARTIN OMANDER:
This sounds amazing.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.460
Let me quickly edit
my settings.py file

00:09:54.460 --> 00:10:00.490
in the other locations to read
in all of our secret values.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:04.800
First, I'll update
my secret key.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.170
Then I'll let the
environment package handle

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:11.070
all of my database settings.

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:15.930
And lastly, I'll read in the
correct static files bucket.

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:19.980
OK, so Craig, you're telling me
that if I run this script now,

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:22.080
it will deploy my app?

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.380
This task that I usually spend
an entire day suffering through

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.810
will be just done?

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:29.550
CRAIG LABENZ: Almost.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.100
Open up that readme, and
you'll see a few extra commands

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:35.130
that we have to run the first
