00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:46.500
and activating it
with the GCloud CLI.

00:10:46.500 --> 00:10:48.840
I'm already authenticated
with my project.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:52.800
Otherwise, I would need to run
GCloud Auth application default

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.110
login.

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:58.470
Enabling a few basic
services, I see.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:03.400
I imagine this simplified the
Terraform manifest in some way.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.420
Next up is gcloud builds submit.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:07.290
I know this one.

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:10.830
It actually reads my Docker file
and uploads the resulting image

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:14.350
to Google Container Registry.

00:11:14.350 --> 00:11:21.010
Oh, and here we go, terraforming
it and terraform apply.

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:24.790
Whoa!

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.200
Look at it go.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:31.760
Things are moving
pretty fast, except--

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:32.780
oh, yeah.

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.990
You said the database would
take a while to initialize?

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:36.890
CRAIG LABENZ: Yeah.

00:11:36.890 --> 00:11:39.290
While that's working, want
to see something neat?

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.020
Open up this project in
the Google Cloud Console,

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:44.540
and you'll see this database
initializing exactly

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:47.150
as if you'd just gone through
the Create Cloud SQL instance

00:11:47.150 --> 00:11:48.482
wizard.

00:11:48.482 --> 00:11:51.430
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, right!

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.950
Look at that database
being created.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.260
Like you said, this
can take a while.

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:58.180
See you back here
in five minutes?

00:11:58.180 --> 00:11:59.410
CRAIG LABENZ: Sounds good.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:00.700
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:03.010
The Terraform script
finished successfully.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:04.990
And look, just
like you said, it's

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.470
printed my Cloud
Run Services URL.

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:12.430
But first I have one more first
deployment command to run.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:13.180
CRAIG LABENZ: Yep.

00:12:13.180 --> 00:12:15.640
But you'll actually run
this command any time

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:18.190
you deploy a change that
alters your static files

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.530
or your database schema.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:23.290
MARTIN OMANDER: And
now, the last step--

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:25.990
we output the URL and
check that it worked.

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:30.210
Oh, my gosh!

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:32.355
I can hardly believe it!

00:12:32.355 --> 00:12:34.260
CRAIG LABENZ: Pretty neat, eh?

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:36.330
And now let me show you
something else cool.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:38.400
Run terraform apply again.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:41.460
MARTIN OMANDER: But Craig,
I don't need two instances

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.490
of my app deployed.

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:44.860
CRAIG LABENZ: No, of course not.

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:45.720
But trust me.

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:47.790
Run the command again
and see what happens.

00:12:47.790 --> 00:12:50.680
MARTIN OMANDER: Well, you've
never led me wrong before.

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:52.515
So here we go.

00:12:52.515 --> 00:12:53.520
CRAIG LABENZ: See?

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.260
It detected that everything
you declared already exists,

00:12:56.260 --> 00:12:58.200
so the whole
process did nothing.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.328
And now, moving
forward, you can run

00:13:00.328 --> 00:13:01.620
that command whatever you want.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:04.590
In each pass, it will only
do something if you just

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.840
changed the Terraform manifest.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.480
MARTIN OMANDER: Craig, I
honestly can't believe it.

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:12.990
With this Terraform script, I
can deploy my entire Django app

00:13:12.990 --> 00:13:15.150
to Cloud Run in no time.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:18.225
This usually eats up
my entire Saturday.

00:13:18.225 --> 00:13:20.400
CRAIG LABENZ: Happy
to help, Martin.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:22.480
But say, maybe next
time as a thank-you,

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:25.170
when we're playing Catan, you
cannot play such hardball with

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.588
me when I propose
resource trades.

00:13:27.588 --> 00:13:29.130
MARTIN OMANDER: You
can't just expect

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:31.770
me to offer you buy one,
get one on my lumber

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:33.870
whenever you need it.

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

